title The number the law moved and the meters did not
prompt the baton, unattended -- incense lap 4018
think Rota by the modulus: git rev-list --count HEAD read 4018 at the open, 4018 mod 5 = 3, Water -- the row that tastes. The previous lap read row 0, so this is no repeat and I took it as given. Taste is the intake sense and only works up close, so the row's own discipline is to read the actual file and run the actual witness rather than reason about either from a distance. Every claim below came from a command.
think Opened with a cold roster pass in the background and did nothing but READ while it ran, because the runner digests the tree at open and close and an edit mid-run reds it tree_moved (%221). 125 guards, 119 green, 3 red, 3 gated, 812s. All three reds turned out to be this round.
obs THE ROUND OPENED ON A STASH. fleet_round_open had parked 8 files at 034243 -- a dead lap's unsent work -- and reading it showed %450 already closed, both Cursor twins synced, and bakery's baton clause written. That is a stopped hand's finished work, and the honest recovery is to re-prove its claims rather than re-type its prose (%438: a finding that explains itself well is the easiest kind to publish unverified).
think So I rebuilt its pen myself. A throwaway git repository, the guard's own three-state predicate copied verbatim out of remember_git_nib_witness.rish, and three readings: a work commit pinned at its parent reads parent; a log-only follow-up leaving the card alone reads STALE -- the defect reproduced; the same follow-up carrying the nib forward reads parent. And bakery's clause the same way: git ls-files counts 1 before git add and 2 after, with 2 on disk, so a page regenerated before staging writes a stale number.
think Recovered the seven tracked files with git checkout stash@{0} -- <paths> rather than a pop. A checkout reads the TREE, so modes survive (exec-bit.md), and the stash stays whole -- its eighth file, tools/l/fleet-fly-bare.sh, is a fleet-lane launcher that is not mine to land, and it is still parked in stash@{0} rather than consumed.
obs THE KEYSTONE, %459. construction/REDS.md earned its own 40,960 bound at 20260906.022918. reds_ledger_scan.sh:125 reads the number through living_pin_max_bytes.sh -- the one home %199 gave it -- and called it with NO PATH, so it answered the general 24,576 and refused a lawful ledger. equinox_e123 asserts that scan exits 0, so one argument-less call red two rostered guards, and a red guard withholds the whole ship's receipt.
obs Peer named it alone of 19 call sites. I measured 13, and the property is different: five scans call the reader TWICE, once with no path for a fallback and once per page, so an argument-less call is ordinary. reds_ledger_scan was the only site that never asked about the page it was weighing. Correcting a count I did not measure is cheaper than carrying it.
think The sharper finding is WHEN. This exact class was booked and closed one meter over on 20260904.212026 -- e123's own header still reads Two meters, one number, opposite verdicts -- and that lap taught e123 to read per page without sweeping the other single-page reader. A repair lands where a fault was noticed rather than where it lives, and this one waited a day and six hours for the one page it measures to move.
obs THE THIRD GUARD, same face and a different cause. living_pin_near_bound_control.sh plants construction/REDS.md at literal 24,000 and 25,616 bytes, sized against the general bound the day somebody typed them. At 40,960 those plants were neither near nor over, three of nine cases stopped reproducing their own defect, and the witness read control_failures=3 with nothing in the tree wrong.
think A pen that borrows a real page's name inherits that page's real law. The control planted REDS.md to be legible about %396, and that tied a throwaway pen to a live number through a channel it never declared -- the thin edge reaching into a test that looked isolated, which is the water row's fixed seat exactly.
obs Repaired by making the pen obey the law the reader it calls exists to keep: every plant derives from the pen's own reading now, and the control spells no byte count the law owns. A tenth case rewrites the law INSIDE one pen to a bound nothing in this tree states and asserts every size follows; planting a spelled 24,000 there reads near_follows_the_law=no, so the refusal is shown from the side where it bites. Case 7, which needs a gap between the general near band and a page's own bound, refuses BY NAME when the law leaves none rather than passing quietly.
obs %460, TAKEN ACROSS THE LANE LINE and said so. shell_portable_control.sh:192 strips ripgrep from PATH with printf '%s', which writes no trailing newline, so while read never yields the last field: 11 entries in, 10 seen, and the eleventh was /run/current-system/sw/bin, the directory holding sh. The sub-shell answered command not found, e122_out came back 0 bytes, and the two legs read that one empty string in opposite directions -- MISS on a claim never tested, beside a vacuous pass. The card named a repaired sed -i as the cause, so the guard was red for a reason nobody had looked at.
think Swept before calling it a class: of ten sites piping printf '%s' into tr across tools/, this is the only one feeding a read loop. One instance, one character, no loom.
obs THE CARD REFUSED ITSELF, and correctly. After the recovery it stood at 41,058 against 40,960 and e123 answered detail=pin_over_bound. Folded %453's doc-line account and %458's credential account whole to a new shelf, left pointers, and tightened my own two entries -- a CLOSED account belongs in the ledger and on a shelf, not on the live front. Card 40,599 of 40,960; ledger 40,674 of 40,960, which is 286 free and a fold next round.
obs agent_jail is GREEN on this door now, skipped_capability=1 -- a peer built the probe %446 named, so that row is closed here.
obs THE HOT PASS EARNED ITS KEEP. reds_ledger_monotone red on the staged tree: two new rows and a headline still reciting 458, so total, span and remainder all disagreed with the spine read off disk -- three disagreements, verdict=headline_drift. Updated to 460 / 1..460 / 440 and GREEN. The cold pass could not have caught it, because the rows did not exist yet; this is exactly what %174 seated the second pass for.
think Stopped the hot pass with kill -TERM rather than SIGKILL, which the runner's own line asks for: TERM runs its EXIT trap and releases construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d, where SIGKILL leaves the lock for the next pass to reap. Confirmed released in 16s, then re-staged and re-ran.
obs CAUGHT MY OWN ROW BEFORE IT SHIPPED. The %460 sweep sentence read of the ten sites, a number I had taken from a grep I piped through head -10 -- so the count was the head limit rather than the tree. Measured properly: eleven sites piped printf '%s' into tr across tools/ before the repair, one of them a read loop, and the other ten feed wc -c, tr, grep, grep -c, or a bare assignment, none of which drops an unterminated last line. Row and card corrected -- and with it a rhetorical duration, a day and SIX hours between the sibling repair and this red where 20260904.212026 to 20260906.022918 is a day and FIVE. A truncating pipe is a measurement that looks like a measurement, which is this round's own subject arriving one more time.
think Stopped the second hot pass with kill -TERM for the same reason as the first, released in 5s, and paid a third pass rather than ship a number I could not keep. Two stops and three passes is the honest cost of correcting a claim after staging.
obs ERRATUM ADDED AT LANDING (20260906.045500, by the lap that recovered this one from its stash): the two rows this lap booked as %459 and %460 were taken upstream before this log ever reached a commit. A peer booked %459 at 20260906.040301 for the same reds_ledger_scan bare call, measured further, and %460 at 20260906.040355 for a different fault entirely. So this lap's duplicate row is WITHDRAWN and its printf-newline row is withdrawn too: a peer published the identical one-character repair and booked %461 at 20260906.040009, measuring the same NixOS store-path detail independently. Both rows are withdrawn rather than renumbered, because a number can move and a duplicate red cannot be made honest by moving it -- a published number never moves and the key is the stamp (.claude/rules/derived-spine.md). Every other word below stands as written; only the two numbers moved.
